# DEBUG_LOG.md Protocol (Android edition)

Mirrors `./debugging.mdc` from the macOS project, retargeted to Kotlin.

Every time you fix a build error, compile warning you treated as an
error, runtime crash, ANR, flaky test, or logic bug, append a
structured entry to `DEBUG_LOG.md` at the project root **before**
telling the user the fix is complete. The entry and the fix live in
the same commit.

## When the log starts

* `DEBUG_LOG.md` is initialised during Phase 4 (hardening) with
  `DL-000 — Initial v1 green build` as the seed entry.
* From Phase 4 onward, every bug fix — build error, compile-warning-
  treated-as-error, runtime crash, ANR, flaky test, or logic bug —
  gets a DL entry in the same commit as the fix. No exceptions.

## Entry template

```
### DL-XXX — [Short Title]

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Date** | YYYY-MM-DD |
| **Severity** | Build Error / Runtime Crash / ANR / Logic Bug / Flaky Test / Warning-as-Error |
| **File(s)** | `path/to/File.kt` |
| **Symptom** | What the user saw or what failed |
| **Root Cause** | Why it happened (1–3 sentences; name the misconception, not just the line) |
| **Fix** | What was changed (reference commit SHA if available) |
| **Prevention Rule** | How to avoid this in the future — written as an imperative rule |
```

## Rules

1. **Sequence**: Number entries DL-001, DL-002, DL-003... Never skip
   numbers; never reuse a number.
2. **Never skip**: Even trivial fixes get logged. A one-character typo
   that cost you five minutes is a DL entry.
3. **Before writing code**: Read DEBUG_LOG.md and check whether any
   Prevention Rules apply to what you're about to touch. Call out the
   matching rules in your "Plan" block.
4. **Append only**: Never delete or modify existing entries. If an
   entry turns out wrong, add a new entry that supersedes it and
   cross-reference.
5. **Prevention rule quality**: A good rule is specific and
   checkable. "Be careful with coroutines" is not a rule. "On
   `SpeechRecognizer.startListening()`, always call from the main
   thread; wrap in `withContext(Dispatchers.Main.immediate)` when the
   caller may be on IO" is a rule.
6. **Cross-cutting rules**: When three or more DL entries share the
   same prevention rule, promote it into this `.cursor/rules/`
   directory as its own `.mdc` file so it becomes ambient guidance.

## Example

```
### DL-007 — TTS silently drops long utterances

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Date** | 2026-04-28 |
| **Severity** | Logic Bug |
| **File(s)** | `android-runtime/src/main/java/.../AndroidTtsClient.kt` |
| **Symptom** | Assistant responses over ~4000 chars were truncated to the first sentence during TTS playback. |
| **Root Cause** | `TextToSpeech.speak` has an internal max string length (~4000 chars on most OEMs). We passed the full response in one call. |
| **Fix** | Chunked text on sentence boundaries before calling `speak(..., QUEUE_ADD, ...)`. Chunker lives in `core/tts/TtsChunker.kt`. |
| **Prevention Rule** | Never pass an unbounded string to a platform TTS or STT API. Always chunk on sentence or soft-token boundaries with a known cap (currently 3500 chars) and enqueue sequentially. |
```
